{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No built-in transport fits your protocol. Write your own connection adapter. There are two shapes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"}]},{"type":"text","value":": one request per user message. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#request-scoped-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request-scoped adapters"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"}]},{"type":"text","value":": one channel that stays open for many messages. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#persistent-transports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistent transports"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"request-scoped-adapters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request-scoped adapters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your transport makes one request per user message, implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ConnectConnectionAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":". This is the lowest-level option before a persistent transport:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, type ConnectConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst myAdapter: ConnectConnectionAdapter = {\n  async *connect(messages, data, abortSignal, runContext) {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/chat\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: {\n        \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n        ...runContext?.headers,\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n        runId: runContext?.runId,\n        messages,\n        ...data,\n      }),\n      ...(abortSignal ? { signal: abortSignal } : {}),\n    });\n\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    if (!response.body) throw new Error(\"Response has no body\");\n\n    // Example: newline-delimited JSON. Replace this loop with whatever\n    // framing your wire format uses, yielding one `StreamChunk` per event.\n    const reader = response.body.getReader();\n    const decoder = new TextDecoder();\n    let buffer = \"\";\n    while (true) {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read();\n      if (done) break;\n      buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });\n      const lines = buffer.split(\"\\n\");\n      buffer = lines.pop() ?? \"\";\n      for (const line of lines) {\n        if (line.trim()) {\n          const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(line);\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n};\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({ connection: myAdapter });","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runContext"},{"type":"text","value":" has these fields:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clientTools"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forwardedProps"},{"type":"text","value":": put these in your JSON payload, so that the server can build an AG-UI-compliant response."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":": copy these onto the POST request headers, not into the body."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in fetch and XHR adapters copy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runContext.headers"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rpcStream()"},{"type":"text","value":" do not, because they never get "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runContext"},{"type":"text","value":". If your custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"},{"type":"text","value":" does not copy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"headers"},{"type":"text","value":", it drops BYOK keys ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-byok-*"},{"type":"text","value":"). See "},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/byok","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bring Your Own Key"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime adds the terminal event for you:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"},{"type":"text","value":" stream completes with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":", the runtime adds one."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"},{"type":"text","value":" stream throws, the runtime adds a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"persistent-transports","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistent transports"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A WebSocket, a BroadcastChannel, postMessage between iframes, or a shared worker works in a different way from request and response. You open the channel once. Then you send and receive on it for the full life of the client. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"},{"type":"text","value":" cannot model this, because it expects one async iterable per request."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a resumable WebSocket, use the built-in "},{"type":"link","href":"./websockets","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" adapter. For other persistent channels, implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SubscribeConnectionAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":" (full definition in "},{"type":"link","href":"#the-adapter-interface","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter interface"}]},{"type":"text","value":"):"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\n// subscribe(abortSignal?): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>   (long-lived)\n// send(messages, data?, abortSignal?, runContext?): Promise<void>   (one per user message)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe()"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" calls it once. It returns a long-lived async iterable of every chunk that the channel produces."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send()"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" calls it once per user message, to put a request frame on the channel. It returns when the frame is written. The chunks arrive separately through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime connects the two. The chunks that arrive between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send()"},{"type":"text","value":" and the next terminal event ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":") belong to that run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-websocket-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom WebSocket example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"webSocket()"},{"type":"text","value":" does not fit (a different wire format, no need for resume, or a server that you do not control), write the adapter yourself:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, type SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nfunction websocketConnection(url: string): SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n  const queue: Array<StreamChunk> = [];\n  let pending: ((chunk: StreamChunk | null) => void) | null = null;\n  let closed = false;\n\n  const ready = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n    ws.addEventListener(\"open\", () => resolve(), { once: true });\n  });\n\n  function deliver(chunk: StreamChunk | null) {\n    const resolve = pending;\n    if (resolve) {\n      pending = null;\n      resolve(chunk);\n    } else if (chunk !== null) {\n      queue.push(chunk);\n    }\n  }\n\n  ws.addEventListener(\"message\", (event) => {\n    const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(event.data);\n    deliver(chunk);\n  });\n  ws.addEventListener(\"close\", () => {\n    closed = true;\n    deliver(null);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    async *subscribe(abortSignal) {\n      // Register the abort listener once (not per-iteration) so it can't\n      // accumulate on a long-lived socket.\n      const onAbort = () => deliver(null);\n      abortSignal?.addEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort, { once: true });\n      try {\n        while (!abortSignal?.aborted) {\n          // Drain buffered chunks BEFORE honoring `closed`: a burst of messages\n          // followed by a close event (common within one macrotask) must still\n          // deliver the queued chunks (including a trailing RUN_FINISHED),\n          // otherwise the client would hang waiting for a terminal it dropped.\n          const buffered = queue.shift();\n          if (buffered !== undefined) {\n            yield buffered;\n            continue;\n          }\n          if (closed) return;\n          const chunk = await new Promise<StreamChunk | null>((resolve) => {\n            pending = resolve;\n          });\n          if (chunk === null) return;\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      } finally {\n        abortSignal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\n      }\n    },\n\n    async send(messages, data, _abortSignal, runContext) {\n      await ready;\n      ws.send(\n        JSON.stringify({\n          threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n          runId: runContext?.runId,\n          messages,\n          data,\n        }),\n      );\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: websocketConnection(\"wss://example.com/chat\"),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Your server must emit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" (or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":") at the end of each run. Without it, the client does not know that the assistant turn ended, and it waits forever. See "},{"type":"link","href":"../chat/stream-events","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stream Events"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full event lifecycle."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"when-to-use-a-persistent-transport","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use a persistent transport"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"},{"type":"text","value":" if one or more of these is true:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One connection carries many runs (the chat thread keeps the socket open across messages)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The server pushes chunks outside of a request (presence updates, server-initiated tool calls, broadcast notifications)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want to share one connection across tabs (BroadcastChannel) or workers."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In other cases, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stream()"},{"type":"text","value":". They are simpler, and you do not manage a connection lifecycle."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-adapter-interface","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter interface"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ConnectionAdapter"},{"type":"text","value":" is a union. Give it "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"},{"type":"text","value":", or give it both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"},{"type":"text","value":". Never give it both modes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport type { ModelMessage, StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport interface RunAgentInputContext {\n  threadId: string;\n  runId: string;\n  parentRunId?: string;\n  clientTools?: Array<{ name: string; description: string; parameters: unknown }>;\n  forwardedProps?: Record<string, unknown>;\n  headers?: Record<string, string>;\n}\n\nexport interface ConnectConnectionAdapter {\n  connect(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n}\n\nexport interface SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  subscribe(abortSignal?: AbortSignal): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n  send(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nexport type ConnectionAdapter =\n  | ConnectConnectionAdapter\n  | SubscribeConnectionAdapter;","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Internally, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatClient"},{"type":"text","value":" changes both shapes to one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"},{"type":"text","value":" pair with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalizeConnectionAdapter()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connect"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the client wraps it in an async queue. The wrapped "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send()"},{"type":"text","value":" waits until the active subscriber processes all events or exits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"send"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the client uses them as they are."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass your adapter as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connection"},{"type":"text","value":" and send a message. The chunks from your transport stream into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" like any built-in adapter."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"request-scoped-adapters","text":"Request-scoped adapters","level":2},{"id":"persistent-transports","text":"Persistent transports","level":2},{"id":"custom-websocket-example","text":"Custom WebSocket example","level":3},{"id":"when-to-use-a-persistent-transport","text":"When to use a persistent transport","level":3},{"id":"the-adapter-interface","text":"The adapter interface","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nNo built-in transport fits your protocol. Write your own connection adapter. There are two shapes:\n\n- **`connect`**: one request per user message. See [Request-scoped adapters](#request-scoped-adapters).\n- **`subscribe` / `send`**: one channel that stays open for many messages. See [Persistent transports](#persistent-transports).\n\n## Request-scoped adapters\n\nIf your transport makes one request per user message, implement `ConnectConnectionAdapter`. This is the lowest-level option before a persistent transport:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, type ConnectConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst myAdapter: ConnectConnectionAdapter = {\n  async *connect(messages, data, abortSignal, runContext) {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/chat\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: {\n        \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n        ...runContext?.headers,\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n        runId: runContext?.runId,\n        messages,\n        ...data,\n      }),\n      ...(abortSignal ? { signal: abortSignal } : {}),\n    });\n\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    if (!response.body) throw new Error(\"Response has no body\");\n\n    // Example: newline-delimited JSON. Replace this loop with whatever\n    // framing your wire format uses, yielding one `StreamChunk` per event.\n    const reader = response.body.getReader();\n    const decoder = new TextDecoder();\n    let buffer = \"\";\n    while (true) {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read();\n      if (done) break;\n      buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });\n      const lines = buffer.split(\"\\n\");\n      buffer = lines.pop() ?? \"\";\n      for (const line of lines) {\n        if (line.trim()) {\n          const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(line);\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n};\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({ connection: myAdapter });\n```\n\n`runContext` has these fields:\n\n- `threadId`, `runId`, `clientTools`, `forwardedProps`: put these in your JSON payload, so that the server can build an AG-UI-compliant response.\n- `headers`: copy these onto the POST request headers, not into the body.\n\nThe built-in fetch and XHR adapters copy `runContext.headers`. `stream()` and `rpcStream()` do not, because they never get `runContext`. If your custom `connect` does not copy `headers`, it drops BYOK keys (`x-byok-*`). See [Bring Your Own Key](../advanced/byok).\n\nThe runtime adds the terminal event for you:\n\n- If your `connect` stream completes with no `RUN_FINISHED`, the runtime adds one.\n- If your `connect` stream throws, the runtime adds a `RUN_ERROR`.\n\n## Persistent transports\n\nA WebSocket, a BroadcastChannel, postMessage between iframes, or a shared worker works in a different way from request and response. You open the channel once. Then you send and receive on it for the full life of the client. `connect` cannot model this, because it expects one async iterable per request.\n\nFor a resumable WebSocket, use the built-in [`webSocket()`](./websockets) adapter. For other persistent channels, implement `SubscribeConnectionAdapter` (full definition in [The adapter interface](#the-adapter-interface)):\n\n```typescript\nimport type { SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\n// subscribe(abortSignal?): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>   (long-lived)\n// send(messages, data?, abortSignal?, runContext?): Promise<void>   (one per user message)\n```\n\n- **`subscribe()`**: the `ChatClient` calls it once. It returns a long-lived async iterable of every chunk that the channel produces.\n- **`send()`**: the `ChatClient` calls it once per user message, to put a request frame on the channel. It returns when the frame is written. The chunks arrive separately through `subscribe()`.\n\nThe runtime connects the two. The chunks that arrive between `send()` and the next terminal event (`RUN_FINISHED` or `RUN_ERROR`) belong to that run.\n\n### Custom WebSocket example\n\nIf `webSocket()` does not fit (a different wire format, no need for resume, or a server that you do not control), write the adapter yourself:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, type SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nfunction websocketConnection(url: string): SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n  const queue: Array<StreamChunk> = [];\n  let pending: ((chunk: StreamChunk | null) => void) | null = null;\n  let closed = false;\n\n  const ready = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n    ws.addEventListener(\"open\", () => resolve(), { once: true });\n  });\n\n  function deliver(chunk: StreamChunk | null) {\n    const resolve = pending;\n    if (resolve) {\n      pending = null;\n      resolve(chunk);\n    } else if (chunk !== null) {\n      queue.push(chunk);\n    }\n  }\n\n  ws.addEventListener(\"message\", (event) => {\n    const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(event.data);\n    deliver(chunk);\n  });\n  ws.addEventListener(\"close\", () => {\n    closed = true;\n    deliver(null);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    async *subscribe(abortSignal) {\n      // Register the abort listener once (not per-iteration) so it can't\n      // accumulate on a long-lived socket.\n      const onAbort = () => deliver(null);\n      abortSignal?.addEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort, { once: true });\n      try {\n        while (!abortSignal?.aborted) {\n          // Drain buffered chunks BEFORE honoring `closed`: a burst of messages\n          // followed by a close event (common within one macrotask) must still\n          // deliver the queued chunks (including a trailing RUN_FINISHED),\n          // otherwise the client would hang waiting for a terminal it dropped.\n          const buffered = queue.shift();\n          if (buffered !== undefined) {\n            yield buffered;\n            continue;\n          }\n          if (closed) return;\n          const chunk = await new Promise<StreamChunk | null>((resolve) => {\n            pending = resolve;\n          });\n          if (chunk === null) return;\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      } finally {\n        abortSignal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\n      }\n    },\n\n    async send(messages, data, _abortSignal, runContext) {\n      await ready;\n      ws.send(\n        JSON.stringify({\n          threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n          runId: runContext?.runId,\n          messages,\n          data,\n        }),\n      );\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: websocketConnection(\"wss://example.com/chat\"),\n});\n```\n\n> **Tip:** Your server must emit `RUN_FINISHED` (or `RUN_ERROR`) at the end of each run. Without it, the client does not know that the assistant turn ended, and it waits forever. See [Stream Events](../chat/stream-events) for the full event lifecycle.\n\n### When to use a persistent transport\n\nUse `subscribe` / `send` if one or more of these is true:\n\n- One connection carries many runs (the chat thread keeps the socket open across messages).\n- The server pushes chunks outside of a request (presence updates, server-initiated tool calls, broadcast notifications).\n- You want to share one connection across tabs (BroadcastChannel) or workers.\n\nIn other cases, use `fetchServerSentEvents` or `stream()`. They are simpler, and you do not manage a connection lifecycle.\n\n## The adapter interface\n\nA `ConnectionAdapter` is a union. Give it `connect`, or give it both `subscribe` and `send`. Never give it both modes.\n\n```typescript\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport type { ModelMessage, StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport interface RunAgentInputContext {\n  threadId: string;\n  runId: string;\n  parentRunId?: string;\n  clientTools?: Array<{ name: string; description: string; parameters: unknown }>;\n  forwardedProps?: Record<string, unknown>;\n  headers?: Record<string, string>;\n}\n\nexport interface ConnectConnectionAdapter {\n  connect(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n}\n\nexport interface SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  subscribe(abortSignal?: AbortSignal): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n  send(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nexport type ConnectionAdapter =\n  | ConnectConnectionAdapter\n  | SubscribeConnectionAdapter;\n```\n\nInternally, `ChatClient` changes both shapes to one `subscribe` / `send` pair with `normalizeConnectionAdapter()`:\n\n- **`connect`**: the client wraps it in an async queue. The wrapped `send()` waits until the active subscriber processes all events or exits.\n- **`subscribe` + `send`**: the client uses them as they are.\n\nPass your adapter as `connection` and send a message. The chunks from your transport stream into `messages` like any built-in adapter.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom Transports\nid: transports-custom\norder: 8\ndescription: \"Build your own TanStack AI connection adapter: a request-scoped connect adapter, or a persistent subscribe/send adapter for WebSockets, BroadcastChannel, and workers.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - custom connection adapter\n  - ConnectConnectionAdapter\n  - SubscribeConnectionAdapter\n  - subscribe send\n  - persistent transport\n  - websocket\n  - runContext\n---\n\nNo built-in transport fits your protocol. Write your own connection adapter. There are two shapes:\n\n- **`connect`**: one request per user message. See [Request-scoped adapters](#request-scoped-adapters).\n- **`subscribe` / `send`**: one channel that stays open for many messages. See [Persistent transports](#persistent-transports).\n\n## Request-scoped adapters\n\nIf your transport makes one request per user message, implement `ConnectConnectionAdapter`. This is the lowest-level option before a persistent transport:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, type ConnectConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nconst myAdapter: ConnectConnectionAdapter = {\n  async *connect(messages, data, abortSignal, runContext) {\n    const response = await fetch(\"/api/chat\", {\n      method: \"POST\",\n      headers: {\n        \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n        ...runContext?.headers,\n      },\n      body: JSON.stringify({\n        threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n        runId: runContext?.runId,\n        messages,\n        ...data,\n      }),\n      ...(abortSignal ? { signal: abortSignal } : {}),\n    });\n\n    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    if (!response.body) throw new Error(\"Response has no body\");\n\n    // Example: newline-delimited JSON. Replace this loop with whatever\n    // framing your wire format uses, yielding one `StreamChunk` per event.\n    const reader = response.body.getReader();\n    const decoder = new TextDecoder();\n    let buffer = \"\";\n    while (true) {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read();\n      if (done) break;\n      buffer += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });\n      const lines = buffer.split(\"\\n\");\n      buffer = lines.pop() ?? \"\";\n      for (const line of lines) {\n        if (line.trim()) {\n          const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(line);\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  },\n};\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({ connection: myAdapter });\n```\n\n`runContext` has these fields:\n\n- `threadId`, `runId`, `clientTools`, `forwardedProps`: put these in your JSON payload, so that the server can build an AG-UI-compliant response.\n- `headers`: copy these onto the POST request headers, not into the body.\n\nThe built-in fetch and XHR adapters copy `runContext.headers`. `stream()` and `rpcStream()` do not, because they never get `runContext`. If your custom `connect` does not copy `headers`, it drops BYOK keys (`x-byok-*`). See [Bring Your Own Key](../advanced/byok).\n\nThe runtime adds the terminal event for you:\n\n- If your `connect` stream completes with no `RUN_FINISHED`, the runtime adds one.\n- If your `connect` stream throws, the runtime adds a `RUN_ERROR`.\n\n## Persistent transports\n\nA WebSocket, a BroadcastChannel, postMessage between iframes, or a shared worker works in a different way from request and response. You open the channel once. Then you send and receive on it for the full life of the client. `connect` cannot model this, because it expects one async iterable per request.\n\nFor a resumable WebSocket, use the built-in [`webSocket()`](./websockets) adapter. For other persistent channels, implement `SubscribeConnectionAdapter` (full definition in [The adapter interface](#the-adapter-interface)):\n\n```typescript\nimport type { SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\n// subscribe(abortSignal?): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>   (long-lived)\n// send(messages, data?, abortSignal?, runContext?): Promise<void>   (one per user message)\n```\n\n- **`subscribe()`**: the `ChatClient` calls it once. It returns a long-lived async iterable of every chunk that the channel produces.\n- **`send()`**: the `ChatClient` calls it once per user message, to put a request frame on the channel. It returns when the frame is written. The chunks arrive separately through `subscribe()`.\n\nThe runtime connects the two. The chunks that arrive between `send()` and the next terminal event (`RUN_FINISHED` or `RUN_ERROR`) belong to that run.\n\n### Custom WebSocket example\n\nIf `webSocket()` does not fit (a different wire format, no need for resume, or a server that you do not control), write the adapter yourself:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, type SubscribeConnectionAdapter } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\nimport type { StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nfunction websocketConnection(url: string): SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  const ws = new WebSocket(url);\n  const queue: Array<StreamChunk> = [];\n  let pending: ((chunk: StreamChunk | null) => void) | null = null;\n  let closed = false;\n\n  const ready = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n    ws.addEventListener(\"open\", () => resolve(), { once: true });\n  });\n\n  function deliver(chunk: StreamChunk | null) {\n    const resolve = pending;\n    if (resolve) {\n      pending = null;\n      resolve(chunk);\n    } else if (chunk !== null) {\n      queue.push(chunk);\n    }\n  }\n\n  ws.addEventListener(\"message\", (event) => {\n    const chunk: StreamChunk = JSON.parse(event.data);\n    deliver(chunk);\n  });\n  ws.addEventListener(\"close\", () => {\n    closed = true;\n    deliver(null);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    async *subscribe(abortSignal) {\n      // Register the abort listener once (not per-iteration) so it can't\n      // accumulate on a long-lived socket.\n      const onAbort = () => deliver(null);\n      abortSignal?.addEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort, { once: true });\n      try {\n        while (!abortSignal?.aborted) {\n          // Drain buffered chunks BEFORE honoring `closed`: a burst of messages\n          // followed by a close event (common within one macrotask) must still\n          // deliver the queued chunks (including a trailing RUN_FINISHED),\n          // otherwise the client would hang waiting for a terminal it dropped.\n          const buffered = queue.shift();\n          if (buffered !== undefined) {\n            yield buffered;\n            continue;\n          }\n          if (closed) return;\n          const chunk = await new Promise<StreamChunk | null>((resolve) => {\n            pending = resolve;\n          });\n          if (chunk === null) return;\n          yield chunk;\n        }\n      } finally {\n        abortSignal?.removeEventListener(\"abort\", onAbort);\n      }\n    },\n\n    async send(messages, data, _abortSignal, runContext) {\n      await ready;\n      ws.send(\n        JSON.stringify({\n          threadId: runContext?.threadId,\n          runId: runContext?.runId,\n          messages,\n          data,\n        }),\n      );\n    },\n  };\n}\n\nconst { messages } = useChat({\n  connection: websocketConnection(\"wss://example.com/chat\"),\n});\n```\n\n> **Tip:** Your server must emit `RUN_FINISHED` (or `RUN_ERROR`) at the end of each run. Without it, the client does not know that the assistant turn ended, and it waits forever. See [Stream Events](../chat/stream-events) for the full event lifecycle.\n\n### When to use a persistent transport\n\nUse `subscribe` / `send` if one or more of these is true:\n\n- One connection carries many runs (the chat thread keeps the socket open across messages).\n- The server pushes chunks outside of a request (presence updates, server-initiated tool calls, broadcast notifications).\n- You want to share one connection across tabs (BroadcastChannel) or workers.\n\nIn other cases, use `fetchServerSentEvents` or `stream()`. They are simpler, and you do not manage a connection lifecycle.\n\n## The adapter interface\n\nA `ConnectionAdapter` is a union. Give it `connect`, or give it both `subscribe` and `send`. Never give it both modes.\n\n```typescript\nimport type { UIMessage } from \"@tanstack/ai-client\";\nimport type { ModelMessage, StreamChunk } from \"@tanstack/ai\";\n\nexport interface RunAgentInputContext {\n  threadId: string;\n  runId: string;\n  parentRunId?: string;\n  clientTools?: Array<{ name: string; description: string; parameters: unknown }>;\n  forwardedProps?: Record<string, unknown>;\n  headers?: Record<string, string>;\n}\n\nexport interface ConnectConnectionAdapter {\n  connect(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n}\n\nexport interface SubscribeConnectionAdapter {\n  subscribe(abortSignal?: AbortSignal): AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>;\n  send(\n    messages: UIMessage[] | ModelMessage[],\n    data?: Record<string, any>,\n    abortSignal?: AbortSignal,\n    runContext?: RunAgentInputContext,\n  ): Promise<void>;\n}\n\nexport type ConnectionAdapter =\n  | ConnectConnectionAdapter\n  | SubscribeConnectionAdapter;\n```\n\nInternally, `ChatClient` changes both shapes to one `subscribe` / `send` pair with `normalizeConnectionAdapter()`:\n\n- **`connect`**: the client wraps it in an async queue. The wrapped `send()` waits until the active subscriber processes all events or exits.\n- **`subscribe` + `send`**: the client uses them as they are.\n\nPass your adapter as `connection` and send a message. The chunks from your transport stream into `messages` like any built-in adapter.\n"}
